<section n:imported="components/docs/section"/>
<highlight n:imported="components/docs/highlight"/>
<body>
    <section title="Reactivity">
        Reactive variables in Nijor provide a powerful way to manupulate the DOM with less code ! <br>
        <highlight>Note : Reactivity is still in development phase. Hence, thus feature might be a little buggy in 1% of the cases.</highlight>
    </section>

    <section title="Update UI">
        Reactive variables update the UI. <br>
        Let's learn using a simple example.
        <pre><code>
            &lt;body&gt;
                &lt;h1&gt;Counter&lt;/h1&gt;
                &lt;p&gt;Count is \{@count}&lt;/p&gt; &lt;!-- This tag gets re-rendered whenever the value of @count changes --&gt;
                &lt;p&gt;Double is \{@double}&lt;/p&gt; &lt;!-- This tag gets re-rendered whenever the value of @double changes --&gt;
                &lt;div&gt;
                    &lt;button on:click="Decrement()"&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;
                    &lt;button on:click="ResetCount()"&gt;Reset&lt;/button&gt;
                    &lt;button on:click="Increment()"&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;
                &lt;/div&gt;
            &lt;/body&gt;

            &lt;script&gt;
                // Variables of type @varname are reactive variables. Reactive variables can only be declared inside a .nijor file, not inside a .js file
                
                const @count = 0; // Reactive variables are declared using the const keyword, but their value can be changed.
                const @double = @count * 2; // It's a derived reactive variable ; it's value changes whenever the value of @count changes.
                
                function Increment() \{
                    @count++;
                }
                function Decrement() \{
                    @count--;
                }

                function ResetCount() \{
                    @count = 0;
                }
            &lt;/script&gt;
        </code></pre>
    </section>

    <section title="Gathering input data">
        <highlight>n:ref</highlight> attribute is used for one-way data-bindings of a reactive variable to an input field. Whenever the value of the input field changes, the value of the reactive variable changes, but not the other way round.
        <pre><code>
            &lt;body&gt;
                &lt;input n:ref="@name" type="text" placeholder="Type your name ..."/&gt; &lt;!-- Whenever the value of this input changes, @name will change --&gt;
                &lt;p&gt;Hello \{@name}&lt;/p&gt; &lt;!-- As you type in the input box, the text inside this tag will get updated in real time --&gt;
            &lt;/body&gt;
            &lt;script&gt;
                const @name = '';
            &lt;/script&gt;
        </code></pre>
        <highlight>n:ref</highlight> is useful for gathering data from inputs for form submitions.
          <pre><code>
            &lt;body&gt;
                &lt;input n:ref="@name" type="text" placeholder="Type your name ..."/&gt;
                &lt;input n:ref="@age" type="number" placeholder="Type your age ..."/&gt;
                &lt;button on:click="sendData()"&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;
            &lt;/body&gt;
            &lt;script&gt;
                const @name = '';
                const @age = 0;

                function sendData()\{
                    let data = \{name: @name, age: @age};
                    // fetch(....);
                }
            &lt;/script&gt;
    </section>

    <section title="Subscribe to events">
        You can subscribe to reactive variables. Whenever a reactive variable changes it's value, the specified function will run.
        <pre><code>
            const @msg = 'Hello';
            @msg.subscribe(value=>console.log(value)); // Whenever @msg changes, the new value of @msg will be printed to the console.
            @msg = 'Hi'; // 'Hi' gets printed to the console.
        </code></pre>
    </section>

    <section title="Importing/Exporting Reactive Variables">
        You can import reactive variables from other components to change the UI in that other component. <br><br>
        
        <p>File : src/components/msgbar.nijor</p>
        <pre><code>
            &lt;body&gt;
                &lt;div&gt;
                    &lt;p&gt;Msg is : \{@msg}&lt;/p&gt;
                &lt;/div&gt;
            &lt;/body&gt;
            &lt;script&gt;
                const @msg = 'Hello';
                export \{ @msg };
            &lt;/script&gt;
        </code></pre>

        <p>File : src/pages/input.nijor</p>
        <pre><code>
            &lt;body&gt;
                &lt;div&gt;
                    &lt;input type="text" n:ref="@msg"/&gt;
                &lt;/div&gt;
            &lt;/body&gt;
            &lt;script&gt;
                import \{ @msg } from 'components/msgbar.nijor';
            &lt;/script&gt;
        </code></pre>

    </section>

</body>

